### Is there any evidence that the SARS-CoV-2 virus itself does not cause COVID-19, and instead the disease is solely a result of 5G radiation?
Answer: There is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 does not cause COVID-19 or that the disease results solely from 5G radiation. The search result, which is a compilation of AFP fact-checks, repeatedly debunks claims linking COVID-19 to 5G technology, stating that "scientists say overwhelming evidence shows the virus is generally transmitted from person to person" and that "the World Health Organization (WHO), radiation experts, and health authorities have said there is no evidence that radiation emitted from 5G is harmful to human health" (entries 1340, 953). Moreover, it explicitly rejects the hoax that Italian doctors discovered COVID-19 is caused by a bacterium and exacerbated by 5G, calling it a "hoax" and affirming that "health research confirms the claims are false" (entry 1283).

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/busting-coronavirus-myths

## Final Judgement
### Summary of Key Insights
The fact-check evidence consistently shows that major health authorities (WHO, CDC, FEMA, FCC) explicitly state that 5G does not cause COVID-19. COVID-19 has been reported in countries without 5G networks, and the SARS-CoV-2 virus is the established cause of the disease. The proposed biological mechanism discussed in one source only hypothesizes that 5G radiation might influence existing infections—not cause the virus itself—and no controlled experiments confirm such effects. The timeline of 5G deployment and the pandemic is merely coincidental, not causal. Multiple fact-checking organizations have debunked the claim. There is no peer-reviewed evidence supporting a causal link, and the single retracted paper on this topic was removed for serious flaws.

### Information Still Lacking
No further information is needed, as the evidence is clear and comprehensive. The claim has been explicitly refuted by authoritative scientific and health organizations, and no credible supporting evidence exists.

### Conclusion
The claim that "5G causes COVID-19" is unequivocally false. The overwhelming consensus from global health authorities, virologists, and scientific evidence confirms that COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus, not by 5G radiation. The evidence explicitly and clearly refutes the claim. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Major health authorities including the World Health Organization, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control, and the Federal Emergency Management Agency have explicitly stated that 5G does not cause COVID‑19 ([UCC brief](https://www.ucc.org/daily_covid_19_brief_issue_62/)). COVID‑19 cases were reported in countries without any 5G network, and the disease is caused by the SARS‑CoV‑2 virus, not by radio waves ([The Conversation](https://theconversation.com/conspiracy-theories-about-5g-networks-have-skyrocketed-since-covid-19-139374)). No peer‑reviewed study demonstrates a causal link; a proposed mechanism that 5G radiation might influence viral replication remains hypothetical and unconfirmed by controlled experiments ([European Parliament document](https://www.europarl.europa.eu/doceo/document/P-9-2020-002498_EN.html)). The single paper claiming such an effect was retracted due to serious methodological flaws ([Medical Xpress](https://medicalxpress.com/news/2020-07-linking-5g-covid-swiftly-debunked.html)). Multiple fact‑checking organizations, including AFP, have debunked the claim ([AFP fact‑check compilation](https://factcheck.afp.com/busting-coronavirus-myths)).